      'A. The oldest object widely accepted as a map of the world is a clay tablet from Babylon, made around 600 BCE and now held in the British Museum. It shows the city of Babylon on the Euphrates, surrounded by a circular ocean, beyond which lie triangular regions described as places where ‘the sun is not seen’. Historians caution against judging it as a failed attempt at accuracy. Its maker was almost certainly not trying to help anyone travel; the tablet expressed a view of the cosmos in which Babylon stood at the centre, and in that sense it served its purpose perfectly.',
      'B. The idea that a map should reproduce real distances owes most to Greek scholars working in Alexandria. In the second century CE, Claudius Ptolemy compiled a list of some 8,000 places, each given a latitude and a longitude, together with instructions for projecting the curved surface of the Earth onto a flat sheet. Ptolemy’s figures contained large errors (he underestimated the size of the globe and stretched Asia far to the east), but his method was sound. Lost to western Europe for centuries, his Geography was translated into Latin in about 1406 and influenced later navigators, possibly including Columbus, whose confidence that Asia lay a short voyage to the west may have rested partly on Ptolemy’s mistakes.',
      'C. Medieval European maps, meanwhile, pursued different goals. The famous Hereford map, drawn around 1300 on a single sheet of calfskin, places Jerusalem at its centre and fills the known world with scenes from the Bible, classical legends and fabulous creatures. It would have been useless for finding the way from one town to another. Yet it would be a mistake to conclude that medieval people had no practical maps. Sailors in the Mediterranean used portolan charts, which traced coastlines with remarkable precision and were criss-crossed by lines showing compass bearings between ports.',
      'D. The problem that defined modern cartography was how to represent a sphere on a plane. Every solution involves distortion; the only question is what to sacrifice. In 1569 the Flemish geographer Gerardus Mercator published a projection on which any line of constant compass bearing appears as a straight line, an enormous advantage for navigators. The price was a steady exaggeration of area towards the poles, so that Greenland appears roughly the size of Africa, although Africa is in reality about fourteen times larger. In 1973 the German historian Arno Peters promoted an equal-area alternative, arguing that Mercator had encouraged a Europe-centred picture of the world. Many cartographers regarded his campaign as poorly informed, pointing out that similar projections had existed for more than a century.',
      'E. National mapping agencies transformed the discipline in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. France completed the first country-wide survey based on triangulation in the 1780s, a project that occupied four generations of the Cassini family. Britain’s Ordnance Survey, founded in 1791 partly for military reasons, eventually produced maps detailed enough to show individual buildings. Such surveys required thousands of careful measurements of the angles between hilltops and church towers, and an error of only a few metres over hundreds of kilometres was considered a triumph.',
      'F. Satellite positioning and digital mapping have made precise location almost free, and today billions of people carry a map in their pocket that redraws itself as they walk. Some geographers worry that this convenience comes at a cost. Studies in which drivers navigated either with turn-by-turn directions or with a paper map suggest that those relying on the device form a weaker mental picture of the area, although the size and duration of the effect are still debated. Others point out that maps have always shaped how people see space. The Babylonian scribe and the smartphone designer, they argue, face the same basic choice about what to show, what to leave out, and whose world to place at the centre.',
