      'A. Coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, yet they are thought to support around a quarter of all marine species at some stage of their lives. They also protect coastlines from waves, and the fisheries and tourism they sustain are worth tens of billions of dollars a year. Since the 1980s, however, reefs have suffered a series of mass bleaching events, in which unusually warm water causes corals to expel the microscopic algae that live in their tissues and supply most of their food. A bleached coral is not dead, but if high temperatures persist for several weeks it may starve. Between 2009 and 2018, according to one global assessment, the world lost roughly 14 per cent of its living coral.',
      'B. Faced with these losses, scientists and volunteers have turned to active restoration. The most widespread technique borrows directly from horticulture. Small fragments are snapped from healthy colonies and grown on underwater structures, frames of metal or plastic pipe that resemble washing lines or trees, until they are large enough to be transplanted onto damaged reef. Because many branching corals naturally reproduce by fragmentation when storms break them, the method takes advantage of an ability the animals already possess. In the Florida Keys, one programme has planted more than 100,000 corals grown in this way since 2007.',
      'C. A newer approach, known as micro-fragmentation, was discovered largely by accident. In 2006 the marine biologist David Vaughan, working at a laboratory in Florida, noticed that a colony he had broken into tiny pieces by mistake had regrown far faster than expected. Slow-growing massive corals, which might normally take decades to reach the size of a football, could be cut into pieces of one or two square centimetres, and these fragments then grew rapidly and fused together when placed side by side. The discovery raised the prospect of restoring reef-building species that had previously seemed too slow to be worth farming.',
      'D. Critics, however, point to the problem of scale. The Great Barrier Reef alone covers some 344,000 square kilometres, whereas most restoration projects are measured in hundreds or at most thousands of square metres. At current costs, which one review estimated at anything from a few thousand to several million dollars per hectare, replanting even a small fraction of degraded reef would be unaffordable. There is also a danger, some ecologists warn, that highly visible restoration projects give the impression that the damage can simply be repaired, distracting attention from the underlying cause, which is the warming of the oceans.',
      'E. Supporters accept that gardening alone cannot save reefs, but argue that it can buy time and preserve genetic diversity. Some laboratories are now deliberately breeding corals that tolerate heat. At one Australian marine research institute, scientists have crossed corals from the warmer northern parts of the Great Barrier Reef with those from cooler southern waters, producing offspring that survive higher temperatures than their southern parents. Others are experimenting with changing the algae that live inside corals. Such ‘assisted evolution’ is controversial, since it is difficult to predict how altered organisms will behave once released into the open sea.',
