    id: 'rt-23',
    title: 'Farming Upwards',
    topic: 'Agriculture and technology',
    minutes: 15,
    paragraphs: [
      'A. By 2050 the world’s population is expected to approach ten billion, and roughly two thirds of those people will live in cities. Feeding them with conventional agriculture would, on some estimates, require additional farmland equal to the area of Brazil. It was this prospect that led Dickson Despommier, a professor of public health at Columbia University, to popularise the idea of the vertical farm in the late 1990s. Working with his students, he imagined skyscrapers in which crops would be grown floor upon floor, close to the consumers who would eat them and entirely protected from drought, floods and pests.',
      'B. Today’s vertical farms are generally less dramatic than Despommier’s towers. Most occupy converted warehouses, shipping containers or old factory buildings, where plants grow on stacked shelves under light-emitting diodes. Instead of soil, the roots sit in trays of circulating nutrient solution, a method known as hydroponics, or are sprayed with a fine mist. Because everything is controlled, from temperature and humidity to light and carbon dioxide, growers can harvest lettuce every few weeks throughout the year. Operators claim to use up to 95 per cent less water than field farming, since moisture evaporating from the plants is captured and reused.',
      'C. The weakness of the model lies in its energy use. In a field, the sun provides light for free; indoors, every unit of light must be paid for. One study by researchers in the north-eastern United States estimated that producing lettuce in a vertical farm consumed several times more energy per kilogram than growing it in a greenhouse, and the difference was far greater when compared with an open field. Light-emitting diodes have become considerably more efficient over the past decade, but many analysts believe that physical limits on converting electricity into light mean that further gains will be modest. Cooling adds to the bill as well, since the lamps themselves produce heat that must be removed.',
      'D. Economics has already claimed several prominent casualties. Between 2022 and 2023 a number of vertical farming companies in the United States and Europe went bankrupt or closed facilities, among them firms that had raised hundreds of millions of dollars from investors. Rising electricity prices following the energy crisis of 2022 were a major factor, but critics also point to a more basic problem: the crops that suit vertical farms, chiefly leafy greens and herbs, are low in calories and relatively cheap, which limits how much growers can charge.',
      'E. Supporters argue that such failures reflect overly ambitious expansion rather than a flaw in the concept. They note that vertical farms make most sense in particular circumstances: in cities with extreme climates, such as Dubai or Singapore, which import the great majority of their fresh food; in places where land is exceptionally expensive; or where consumers are willing to pay more for produce grown without pesticides and delivered within hours of harvest. Singapore, which produces less than ten per cent of its own food, has set a target of raising that share to 30 per cent, and vertical farms form part of that strategy.',
      'F. Few experts now believe that vertical farming will replace conventional agriculture for staple crops. Growing wheat indoors, one analysis concluded, would cost many times more than buying it on world markets. A more likely future, according to most observers, is a niche but valuable role: supplying fresh salads and herbs to cities, reducing losses from long-distance transport, and serving as a testing ground for techniques that may later be applied in greenhouses. Several companies have already moved in this direction, licensing their technology to greenhouse operators rather than running large farms of their own. The skyscraper farm, in short, may remain a vision rather than a blueprint.',
    ],
