    id: 'rt-24',
    title: 'The Architecture of Habit',
    topic: 'Psychology',
    minutes: 15,
    paragraphs: [
      'A. Psychologists estimate that a large share of everyday behaviour is performed more or less automatically. In one frequently cited study, researchers at Duke University asked participants to record what they were doing at regular intervals throughout the day and found that around 43 per cent of their actions were repeated almost daily in the same context, often while they were thinking about something else. Habits, in this sense, are not simply things we do often. They are behaviours triggered by a particular situation, such as a place, a time of day or a preceding action, with little conscious decision involved.',
      'B. Research on animals laid the foundation for this view. In the 1990s, neuroscientists at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology recorded activity in the brains of rats as they learned to run through a maze to reach a piece of chocolate. At first, activity in a region called the basal ganglia was high throughout the run, as the animals explored. As the route became familiar, activity became concentrated at the beginning and end of the run, while the middle section required little attention. The researchers interpreted this as evidence that the brain packages a sequence of actions into a single routine, freeing mental resources for other tasks.',
      'C. How long does a habit take to form? Popular books frequently claim 21 days, a figure that appears to derive from a plastic surgeon’s observations in the 1960s about how quickly patients adjusted to their new appearance. A more systematic study, conducted by Phillippa Lally and colleagues at University College London in 2009, followed 96 volunteers, each of whom chose a new behaviour, such as drinking water after breakfast or going for a run before dinner. On average, it took 66 days before the behaviour reached its maximum level of automaticity, but the range was enormous, from 18 days to an estimated 254. Missing a single day, the researchers found, did not significantly affect the process.',
      'D. These findings have practical implications. Because habits depend on context, one of the most effective ways to change them may be to change the environment rather than to rely on willpower. A study of students who transferred to a new university found that those with strong habits of exercising or watching television were more likely to alter them after the move, because the familiar cues had disappeared. Some researchers call this the ‘habit discontinuity’ effect and suggest that major life events, such as moving house or starting a new job, offer a window in which new behaviours can be established more easily.',
      'E. Not all psychologists accept that habits are as powerful as this research implies. Critics point out that many studies rely on people’s own reports of how automatic a behaviour feels, which may be unreliable. Others argue that the distinction between habitual and goal-directed behaviour is less sharp than it appears, since people can interrupt even deeply ingrained routines when their goals change. There is also debate about whether some supposedly habitual behaviours, such as repeatedly checking a smartphone, are better described as responses to rewards that arrive unpredictably.',
      'F. Despite these disagreements, the research has influenced policy. Governments and companies increasingly design environments that make healthy choices the default, for instance by placing fruit rather than sweets near supermarket checkouts, or by enrolling employees automatically in pension schemes. Supporters regard this as a sensible use of psychology; opponents worry that it can become manipulation when people are not aware that their behaviour is being shaped. What most researchers agree on is that intentions alone rarely change behaviour, and that lasting change is most likely when a new action is repeated consistently in a stable setting.',
    ],
